I was referring more to the concern in general voiced in this thread and 
an earlier thread that conducts in themselves are too spoily, since they 
might give an unspoiled player ideas what to try. For example, a 
hornless conduct that says 'never applied a unicorn horn' might make an 
unspoiled player think 'hey, I should try applying a unicorn horn next 
time'. However, in this case there is clearly no time in the game where 
you could start including this conduct (like in the above Medusa 
example) other than ascension. The same goes for people who think 'never 
wished for an artifact' is too spoily, since it tips you to the 
existance of both wishes and artifacts.

My idea was to make 'disclose: -c' the default on a fresh install. This 
would mean a new player wouldn't see any conducts, and a player who 
wanted to play with conducts would have to change the settings file.

But now that I think about it, I think the guidebook itself is much 
spoilier. Take, for example, the section about vegan diets:

"Metal  or another normally indigestible material eaten while 
polymorphed into a creature that can digest it is also considered vegan 
food."

This is a fairly major spoiler about eating rings and such. There are 
other things in there that can be considered spoilers.

I haven't tried your patch yet, btw, although I like it. The only thing 
I don't like is the increase of the follow-radius. I try to apply only 
patches which don't change gameplay and don't break any compatibility, 
which yours doesn't fit (though I assume it doesn't break any 
compatibility?).

